 ## Unreleased
 
-- **Hub follow-ups receive newly committed inputs after merge/continue** (V-1125). Before planning each bound worker upload, the broker fast-forwards its existing branch to the live declared base under the repository lock. Divergence, WIP, in-progress Git operations and input collisions refuse before provider submission; no reset, rebase or ownership expansion. Refresh checks the retained remote baseline before replacing files, preserving unsynced remote work and retrying already delivered bytes safely. Explicit schema-2 `continue` retains the hub copy, input provenance and thread history instead of releasing them during post-merge cleanup. Upload/data guards remain in force. Python activation follows the existing idle watcher; existing loaded sessions must rebuild their backend to acquire the live base binding.
+- **Hub follow-ups can continue ready, unmerged worker commits after the base advances** (V-1129; `app/hub_workspace.py`). Optional source refresh now visibly skips diverged history or local changes and uploads from the existing task branch, explicitly reporting that base inputs were not refreshed. Branch/HEAD, staged and unstaged data, input provenance and retained thread history stay intact; clean-behind branches still fast-forward under the repository writer lock and deliver new committed inputs. Binding/Git inspection errors, active Git operations, immutable-input violations, actual fast-forward overwrite collisions and remote WIP conflicts still refuse. No merge, rebase, reset, stash, ownership or quota change. Python activation follows the existing idle watcher.
+
+- **Hub follow-ups receive newly committed inputs after merge/continue** (V-1125). Before planning each bound worker upload, the broker attempts to fast-forward its existing branch to the live declared base under the repository lock. The original divergence/WIP refusal was corrected by V-1129: those states visibly skip the optional refresh and retain the existing branch; in-progress Git operations and collisions in an attempted refresh still refuse before provider submission. No reset, rebase or ownership expansion. Refresh checks the retained remote baseline before replacing files, preserving unsynced remote work and retrying already delivered bytes safely. Explicit schema-2 `continue` retains the hub copy, input provenance and thread history instead of releasing them during post-merge cleanup. Upload/data guards remain in force. Python activation follows the existing idle watcher; existing loaded sessions must rebuild their backend to acquire the live base binding.
 
 - **Local Codex, including orchestrators, follows the current Claude quota pacing** (V-1121; `app/quota_gate.py`, `app/session.py`, `app/manager.py`). Weekly-only Pro windows and dual-window plans use the existing day/night line, policy-history envelope, reset and general hard stop. The default Sol reserve and Luna/Spark line exemptions are removed; explicit owner overrides and ceilings remain. Hub admission stays in its separate pool, and Claude's authorized paths remain unchanged. New Codex turns check quota at creation, delivery preflight and submission, including system wakes. Existing-session exemption uses its actual runtime, preserving Claude timer/queue recovery after a legacy model leaves the catalog while still requiring admission for local Codex with an unknown model name. Deferred deliveries and blocked in-memory Codex batches use the existing durable `WAITING_QUOTA` queue and quota sweep; quota-source errors remain unknown/fail-open. The dashboard uses each pool's own window and labels the orchestrator exemption as Claude-only. Python activation follows the existing idle-restart lifecycle; no deployment or restart is performed.
 - **Hub-only ordinary agent dispatch** (V-1119). Agent worker creation and model changes refuse native subscription routes independently of stored model flags; manual dashboard selection and persisted-session recovery remain available. Explicit Claude API-credit workflow tasks retain opt-in admission. The one-shot workflow dispatcher refuses native subscription calls and explains that hub work needs an ordinary worker because no hub adapter exists. The canonical routing module assigns local orchestration to Codex, keeps `hubsol` as the general default and selects hub Sonnet/Opus for any creative work, with existing reason metadata and data/credential/quota guards. No automatic fallback, thread migration or service restart. Python activation follows the normal service lifecycle.
